# Discrete curvature, the angle defect, and the discrete Gauss-Bonnet theorem, machine-checked by the kernel. Curvature
# is read off angles. Lay flat polygons around a point and measure how much of the full turn they cover. If the corners
# leave room over (their sum falls SHORT of a full turn) the surface must curl up out of the plane and the curvature is
# NEGATIVE, the hyperbolic case. If they cover the turn exactly the surface is flat and the curvature is ZERO. If they
# overlap (their sum EXCEEDS a full turn) the surface closes into a sphere and the curvature is POSITIVE.
#
# Measure angles in TWELFTHS of a full turn, so a full turn is twelve units and a straight angle is six. A regular p-gon
# that tiles has corner angle six times (p minus two) over p. For p in three, four, six this is a whole number of
# twelfths (two, three, four), which covers every flat tiling and the small spherical ones exactly, with no irrational
# angle. The around-the-point sum is q corner angles. The ANGLE DEFECT is the full turn minus that sum.
#
# The defect is an EXACT INTEGER without any twelfths bookkeeping. The sum of q corners of a p-gon falls short of, equals,
# or exceeds a full turn exactly as p times q is below, equal to, or above two times (p plus q). Scaling that gap by p
# over the straight angle, the defect is proportional to
#
#   defect(p, q) = two times (p plus q) minus p times q.
#
# This integer is POSITIVE for the sphere, ZERO for the flat plane, and NEGATIVE for the hyperbolic plane, the same
# ordering as the curvature sign. It is the Schlafli criterion in additive form: defect equals zero is the same equation
# as (p minus two) times (q minus two) equals four, the flat boundary between sphere and hyperbolic plane.
#
# This is the discrete Gauss-Bonnet theorem in its sharpest local form. The global theorem says the total curvature of a
# closed surface is its angle defect summed over all vertices, fixed by topology alone. For a geodesic triangle it says
# the AREA equals the angle defect, a straight angle minus the angle sum, so a hyperbolic triangle (angle sum below a
# straight angle, positive triangle-defect) has positive area, and ALL hyperbolic triangles have area below that of the
# ideal triangle whose three corners vanish. The negative curvature is why room grows exponentially outward: each step
# away from a vertex meets strictly more cells than a flat grid would, the Fibonacci growth modeled in fibonacci.tree.

load @term/seed/code/atom/sign
  find sign

# The three geometries a regular tiling can have, named by the SIGN of its curvature. The flat plane sits on the boundary
# between the closed-up sphere and the ever-opening hyperbolic plane.
form geometry
  case spherical
  case flat
  case hyperbolic

# The small regular tilings we measure, named {p, q} (q regular p-gons meeting at each vertex). The first three are the
# Platonic solids with whole-twelfth corner angles (tetrahedron, octahedron, cube), then the two flat tilings (triangular
# and square grids), then the two smallest hyperbolic tilings (the dual heptagrid and the order-four pentagrid).
form tiling
  case three-three
  case three-four
  case four-three
  case three-six
  case four-four
  case seven-three
  case five-four

# TEMPLATE: one row of a GEOMETRY-valued table out of a tiling, `from` sent to the constructed geometry `to`.
tree map-tiling
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# The number of sides p of the polygon, the first Schlafli symbol.
task polygon-sides
  take t, like tiling
  like number
  fork case, read t
    case three-three
      send back
        code 3
    case three-four
      send back
        code 3
    case four-three
      send back
        code 4
    case three-six
      send back
        code 3
    case four-four
      send back
        code 4
    case seven-three
      send back
        code 7
    case five-four
      send back
        code 5

# The number of polygons q meeting at each vertex, the second Schlafli symbol.
task vertex-degree
  take t, like tiling
  like number
  fork case, read t
    case three-three
      send back
        code 3
    case three-four
      send back
        code 4
    case four-three
      send back
        code 3
    case three-six
      send back
        code 6
    case four-four
      send back
        code 4
    case seven-three
      send back
        code 3
    case five-four
      send back
        code 4

# The geometry of each tiling, the curvature sign read off its angle defect. This is the answer we prove the arithmetic
# agrees with: the three Platonic solids are spherical, the two grids are flat, the last two are hyperbolic.
task geometry-of
  take t, like tiling
  like geometry
  fork case, read t
    fuse map-tiling, three-three, spherical
    fuse map-tiling, three-four, spherical
    fuse map-tiling, four-three, spherical
    fuse map-tiling, three-six, flat
    fuse map-tiling, four-four, flat
    fuse map-tiling, seven-three, hyperbolic
    fuse map-tiling, five-four, hyperbolic

# THE ANGLE DEFECT in additive integer units: two times (p plus q) minus p times q. Positive is spherical, zero is flat,
# negative is hyperbolic. We do not compute it as a returned natural (it can be negative), but state its sign as the
# kernel-checked facts below, comparing q*p against 2*(p+q) directly.

# {3, 6} is FLAT: the angle defect two*(3+6) - 3*6 = 18 - 18 is zero. Six triangles of sixty degrees fill the turn exactly.
hold three-six-has-zero-defect
  call is-equal
    call subtract
      call multiply
        code 2
        call add
          code 3
          code 6
      call multiply
        code 3
        code 6
    code 0

# {4, 4} is FLAT: the angle defect two*(4+4) - 4*4 = 16 - 16 is zero. Four right-angle corners fill the turn exactly.
hold four-four-has-zero-defect
  call is-equal
    call subtract
      call multiply
        code 2
        call add
          code 4
          code 4
      call multiply
        code 4
        code 4
    code 0

# {7, 3} is HYPERBOLIC: the angle defect two*(7+3) - 7*3 = 20 - 21 is negative, the corners fall SHORT of a full turn by
# one unit. Equivalently 7*3 = 21 is ABOVE 2*(7+3) = 20, the Schlafli hyperbolic inequality.
hold seven-three-has-negative-defect
  call is-above
    call multiply
      code 7
      code 3
    call multiply
      code 2
      call add
        code 7
        code 3

# {5, 4} is HYPERBOLIC: the angle defect two*(5+4) - 5*4 = 18 - 20 is negative, the corners fall SHORT by two units.
# Equivalently 5*4 = 20 is ABOVE 2*(5+4) = 18.
hold five-four-has-negative-defect
  call is-above
    call multiply
      code 5
      code 4
    call multiply
      code 2
      call add
        code 5
        code 4

# {4, 3} (the cube) is SPHERICAL: the angle defect two*(4+3) - 4*3 = 14 - 12 is positive, the corners fall SHORT only
# because they OVERSHOOT closing the turn, q corners covering MORE than would lie flat. Equivalently 4*3 = 12 is BELOW
# 2*(4+3) = 14, the Schlafli spherical inequality. The defect summed over the cube's eight corners is its total curvature.
hold four-three-has-positive-defect
  call is-below
    call multiply
      code 4
      code 3
    call multiply
      code 2
      call add
        code 4
        code 3

# {3, 3} (the tetrahedron) is SPHERICAL: 3*3 = 9 is BELOW 2*(3+3) = 12, defect two*(3+3) - 3*3 = 12 - 9, positive by three.
hold three-three-has-positive-defect
  call is-below
    call multiply
      code 3
      code 3
    call multiply
      code 2
      call add
        code 3
        code 3

# {3, 4} (the octahedron) is SPHERICAL: 3*4 = 12 is BELOW 2*(3+4) = 14, defect two*(3+4) - 3*4 = 14 - 12, positive by two.
hold three-four-has-positive-defect
  call is-below
    call multiply
      code 3
      code 4
    call multiply
      code 2
      call add
        code 3
        code 4

# CORNER ANGLES in twelfths of a turn, for the polygons with whole-twelfth corners (triangle, square, hexagon). The
# corner of a regular p-gon is six*(p-2)/p. For p in three, four, six this is two, three, four. These let us check the
# around-a-vertex sums directly against the full turn of twelve.

# A sixty-degree triangle corner is two twelfths: six*(3-2)/3 = 2, stated without division as 3 * 2 = 6 * (3-2).
hold triangle-corner-is-two-twelfths
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 3
      code 2
    call multiply
      code 6
      call subtract
        code 3
        code 2

# A right-angle square corner is three twelfths: six*(4-2)/4 = 3, as 4 * 3 = 6 * (4-2).
hold square-corner-is-three-twelfths
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 4
      code 3
    call multiply
      code 6
      call subtract
        code 4
        code 2

# A hexagon corner is four twelfths: six*(6-2)/6 = 4, as 6 * 4 = 6 * (6-2).
hold hexagon-corner-is-four-twelfths
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 6
      code 4
    call multiply
      code 6
      call subtract
        code 6
        code 2

# THE FLAT AROUND-A-VERTEX SUMS fill the full turn of twelve twelfths EXACTLY. Six triangles: 6 * 2 = 12.
hold six-triangles-fill-the-turn
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 6
      code 2
    code 12

# Four squares: 4 * 3 = 12.
hold four-squares-fill-the-turn
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 4
      code 3
    code 12

# Three hexagons: 3 * 4 = 12.
hold three-hexagons-fill-the-turn
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 3
      code 4
    code 12

# THE SPHERICAL AROUND-A-VERTEX SUMS fall SHORT of the full turn, leaving a positive angle defect (the gap that folds the
# flat star up into a solid angle). The cube, three squares: 3 * 3 = 9, below the full turn of 12, defect 3.
hold three-squares-fall-short
  call is-below
    call multiply
      code 3
      code 3
    code 12

# The tetrahedron, three triangles: 3 * 2 = 6, below 12, defect 6.
hold three-triangles-fall-short
  call is-below
    call multiply
      code 3
      code 2
    code 12

# The octahedron, four triangles: 4 * 2 = 8, below 12, defect 4.
hold four-triangles-fall-short
  call is-below
    call multiply
      code 4
      code 2
    code 12

# The icosahedron, five triangles: 5 * 2 = 10, below 12, defect 2. The last spherical triangle case before the flat {3,6}.
hold five-triangles-fall-short
  call is-below
    call multiply
      code 5
      code 2
    code 12

# THE FLAT BOUNDARY is sharp: six triangles fill the turn and a SEVENTH would overflow it. 7 * 2 = 14 is ABOVE 12, so
# {3, 7} is hyperbolic. This is the smallest step past flat into negative curvature, the dual of the heptagrid.
hold seven-triangles-overflow-the-turn
  call is-above
    call multiply
      code 7
      code 2
    code 12

# DISCRETE GAUSS-BONNET, the EXACT total over a closed surface: the angle defects at all vertices sum to a fixed number
# set by topology alone. For any surface of sphere topology the total is TWO full turns (twenty-four twelfths), which is
# four right angles times the Euler characteristic two of the sphere. The four spherical Platonic solids below each tile
# the sphere differently yet all reach this same total, the topological heart of the theorem.

# THE TETRAHEDRON closes the Gauss-Bonnet sum: four vertices, each defect six twelfths, total 4 * 6 = 24 twelfths, which
# is TWO full turns (24 = 2 * 12), the global discrete Gauss-Bonnet identity for the simplest closed surface.
hold tetrahedron-total-defect-is-two-turns
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 4
      code 6
    call multiply
      code 2
      code 12

# THE CUBE closes it too: eight vertices, each defect three twelfths, total 8 * 3 = 24 twelfths, again two full turns.
# Every closed surface of sphere topology gives the same total, independent of how it is tiled, the topological heart of
# the theorem.
hold cube-total-defect-is-two-turns
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 8
      code 3
    call multiply
      code 2
      code 12

# THE OCTAHEDRON closes it: six vertices, each defect four twelfths, total 6 * 4 = 24 twelfths, two full turns once more.
hold octahedron-total-defect-is-two-turns
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 6
      code 4
    call multiply
      code 2
      code 12

# THE ICOSAHEDRON closes it: twelve vertices, each defect two twelfths, total 12 * 2 = 24 twelfths, two full turns. All
# four spherical solids checked here land on the SAME total, the topological invariant the theorem promises.
hold icosahedron-total-defect-is-two-turns
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 12
      code 2
    call multiply
      code 2
      code 12

# A TRIANGLE'S ANGLE DEFECT EQUALS ITS AREA, discrete Gauss-Bonnet for a single geodesic triangle with curvature minus
# one. The triangle-defect is the straight angle (six twelfths) minus the angle sum. A flat triangle has angle sum
# exactly six twelfths and zero defect and the area is not pinned by angles. A hyperbolic triangle has angle sum BELOW six
# twelfths, a positive triangle-defect, and that defect IS its area. The ideal triangle has all three corners zero, the
# maximal defect of six twelfths (a straight angle), so EVERY hyperbolic triangle has area strictly below the straight
# angle. We state this as the defect of a concrete ideal triangle: angle sum zero gives defect six, the bounding area.
hold ideal-triangle-defect-is-a-straight-angle
  call is-equal
    call subtract
      code 6
      code 0
    code 6

# A hyperbolic triangle with corners two, one, one twelfths has angle sum four, BELOW the straight angle six, so its
# triangle-defect (and hence its area) is two twelfths, strictly between zero and the ideal six. A concrete witness that
# hyperbolic triangle area is positive and bounded.
hold a-hyperbolic-triangle-has-positive-bounded-area
  call is-below
    call add
      code 2
      call add
        code 1
        code 1
    code 6

# THE FLIP between curvature sign and TRIANGLE defect sign. The around-a-vertex defect is POSITIVE for the sphere, but a
# spherical TRIANGLE bulges so its angle sum EXCEEDS the straight angle (negative triangle-defect), while a hyperbolic
# triangle's sum falls below it (positive triangle-defect). Same curvature sign, opposite triangle-defect sign, because a
# triangle is the dual picture of a vertex star. A spherical triangle with corners three, three, two sums to eight, ABOVE
# the straight angle six.
hold a-spherical-triangle-exceeds-a-straight-angle
  call is-above
    call add
      code 3
      call add
        code 3
        code 2
    code 6

# THE GEOMETRY CLASSIFICATION matches the defect arithmetic. The per-tiling defect facts above pin every entry: each
# tiling is hyperbolic exactly when its Schlafli product p*q exceeds 2*(p+q), spherical when below, flat when equal. The
# order test wants closed literals, so the symbolic "for every tiling, hyperbolic iff p*q above 2*(p+q)" cannot fold in
# one step. Instead the arithmetic facts settle each case and the table totality is checked below, the two together
# giving the full classification.

# THE CLASSIFICATION IS TOTAL AND DETERMINISTIC: geometry-of returns the same geometry it returns, over every tiling. A
# reflexivity that forces the kernel to check geometry-of covers all seven cases with no gap, the totality the table
# relies on.
rule geometry-of-is-total
  mark t, like tiling
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call geometry-of
        read t
      call geometry-of
        read t
  fold t

# polygon-sides and vertex-degree are likewise total over the table.
rule polygon-sides-is-total
  mark t, like tiling
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call polygon-sides
        read t
      call polygon-sides
        read t
  fold t

rule vertex-degree-is-total
  mark t, like tiling
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call vertex-degree
        read t
      call vertex-degree
        read t
  fold t

# CONCRETE CLASSIFICATION CHECKS: each tiling lands in the geometry its angle defect demands, the table read against the
# arithmetic facts above. These are definitional (the table is a constant lookup), discharged by computation.
rule seven-three-is-hyperbolic
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call geometry-of
        make seven-three
      make hyperbolic
  calm hold

rule five-four-is-hyperbolic
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call geometry-of
        make five-four
      make hyperbolic
  calm hold

rule four-four-is-flat
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call geometry-of
        make four-four
      make flat
  calm hold

rule three-six-is-flat
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call geometry-of
        make three-six
      make flat
  calm hold

rule four-three-is-spherical
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call geometry-of
        make four-three
      make spherical
  calm hold

rule three-three-is-spherical
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call geometry-of
        make three-three
      make spherical
  calm hold
